# ilmNet server — environment example
#
# ⚠️  DEVELOPMENT ONLY. This file is meant for local work (`npm run dev`, the test suites, seeds).
#     In production the server reads its configuration from the *process environment* (systemd
#     EnvironmentFile, `docker compose environment:`, PaaS env vars, `docker run --env-file`) and
#     it refuses to boot when a .env file here would supply NODE_ENV / ENVIRONMENT / ADMIN_TOKEN /
#     CORS_ORIGIN / ADMIN_ALLOW_LOCALHOST. Never place a .env file in the app directory of a
#     production or staging host; server/.dockerignore already keeps it out of the image.
#
# ── Which environment is this? (docs/ENVIRONMENTS.md) ──
# ilmNet has exactly three environments: development, staging and production. ENVIRONMENT names the
# one this process belongs to; it is read from the process environment and may NOT come from a .env
# file on a staging or production boot (the boot is refused if it does).
#
#   development  → local work (this file). NODE_ENV=development.
#   staging      → production-like validation. Requires NODE_ENV=production and the *same*
#                  configuration shape, build and guards as production. Never crawlable
#                  (robots.txt disallows everything, responses carry x-robots-tag: noindex).
#   production   → the live site. NODE_ENV=production.
#
# Examples (process environment — systemd EnvironmentFile, docker compose `environment:`, PaaS):
#   ENVIRONMENT=staging    NODE_ENV=production  ADMIN_TOKEN=<openssl rand -hex 32>
#   ENVIRONMENT=production NODE_ENV=production  ADMIN_TOKEN=<openssl rand -hex 32>
#
# Leaving ENVIRONMENT unset on a NODE_ENV=production boot still resolves to `production` (so an
# existing host keeps working) but the server logs a warning: set it explicitly on every deployment.
# Never set NODE_ENV=staging — staging runs NODE_ENV=production with ENVIRONMENT=staging.
#
# ── Database ──
# local dev (matches docker-compose)
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ilmnet:ilmnet_dev@localhost:5432/ilmnet?schema=public"
# inside docker compose use the service name instead of localhost:
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://ilmnet:ilmnet_dev@postgres:5432/ilmnet?schema=public"

# ── Server ──
PORT=3001
HOST=0.0.0.0
NODE_ENV=development

# ── CORS ──
# Exact browser origins that may call the API, comma separated.
# Production: never use "*" (the server refuses to boot with a wildcard in production).
CORS_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:3000"

# ── Admin protection ──
# Admins sign in at /admin with a username + password (server-side sessions, HttpOnly cookie). The
# legacy shared token below is only a fallback for scripts and CI that have no browser. Since
# Fase 5.3 it is OFF in production unless you opt in with ADMIN_LEGACY_TOKEN=true (in development it
# stays on, so local scripts keep working). Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
ADMIN_TOKEN="change-me-dev-only"

# Explicit switch for the legacy token path (Fase 5.3). Unset = on in development, OFF in
# production. Setting it to true in production requires ADMIN_TOKEN; setting it to false requires at
# least one active admin account, otherwise the server refuses to boot (nobody could sign in).
# ADMIN_LEGACY_TOKEN=true

# Create the first operator (never do this from the browser or with SQL):
#   npm run admin:create -- --username admin --password '<a long, unique password>'
# Also available: admin:password, admin:disable, admin:enable, admin:list.
# Those CLI scripts also accept the password as `ADMIN_PASSWORD=<value>` instead of `--password`,
# which is handy in CI. It is read by the script only — it is not server configuration, and it must
# never be committed or put in a .env file on a production host.

# Session lifetime in minutes (default 720 = 12 hours; clamped to 5 minutes - 30 days). Sessions
# roll on use and are capped at 30 days; changing a password or disabling an account revokes them.
# ADMIN_SESSION_TTL_MINUTES=720

# Local development convenience: same-machine requests (vite proxy, curl, tests) skip the
# token check. Set to "false" to enforce the token even on localhost. Never active in production.
# ADMIN_ALLOW_LOCALHOST=true

# ── YouTube import (optional, server-side only) ──
# Without this key the importer reads the public YouTube watch/playlist pages (it keeps working).
# With a key it uses the official YouTube Data API v3 first: exact durations and publish dates, and
# `status.embeddable` — the one thing scraping cannot tell us, while an unembeddable video would
# create a page with a player that cannot play it. If the API rejects the key, ilmNet falls back to
# the public pages and says so in the admin preview.
#
# NEVER put this in a VITE_* variable, in the database, in the frontend or in a repository: Vite
# inlines every VITE_* value into the public bundle, and only the server talks to YouTube.
# In production set it in the process environment (systemd EnvironmentFile, docker compose
# environment:, PaaS env var), exactly like ADMIN_TOKEN.
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# ── Reverse proxy: wie mag forwarded headers zetten? (Fase 5.2) ──
# De API leest x-forwarded-for / -proto / -host ALLEEN als je hier zegt welke proxy vertrouwd mag
# worden. Default (leeg/false): niets wordt vertrouwd — de socket-verbinding bepaalt het IP, en
# x-forwarded-* wordt genegeerd. Zet dit op het adres/CIDR van je proxy, of op "true" als de API
# uitsluitend via de proxy bereikbaar is (poort niet publiek, bind op 127.0.0.1).
#
# Waarom dit geen detail is: een vertrouwde x-forwarded-for bepaalt het IP dat de login-throttle
# gebruikt en dat in admin-sessies/logs belandt; x-forwarded-proto bepaalt of de app HSTS stuurt;
# x-forwarded-host kan (zonder allowlist) het doel van de http→https-redirect sturen.
# TRUST_PROXY=127.0.0.1
# TRUST_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8
# TRUST_PROXY=true
#
# Canonieke origin voor de http→https-redirect (en de eerste HSTS-beslissing). Als dit gezet is,
# komt het redirect-doel nooit uit het request. Zonder PUBLIC_ORIGIN gebruikt de app de hostnamen
# uit CORS_ORIGIN/ALLOWED_HOSTS; staat er niets, dan redirect de app niet (laat het de proxy doen).
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://ilmnet.example
#
# Extra hostnamen die als geldig redirect-doel mogen gelden (kommagescheiden), bijvoorbeeld een
# intern healthcheck-adres. Zelden nodig.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=ilmnet.example,www.ilmnet.example

# ── TLS / HTTPS (Fase 5.1) ──
# HSTS is only sent on requests that really arrive over HTTPS (direct TLS, or a reverse proxy that
# sets `x-forwarded-proto`). Default: one year. 0 disables the header.
# HSTS_MAX_AGE=31536000
#
# HTTP → HTTPS redirect (opt-in, production only). Turn this on when a TLS-terminating proxy forwards
# the original scheme in `x-forwarded-proto`; without that header the app can only ever see `http` and
# would redirect in a loop. Health endpoints stay reachable over plain HTTP so a container
# HEALTHCHECK that talks to the app socket keeps working (server/Dockerfile).
# FORCE_HTTPS=true

# ── Upload storage (custom thumbnails / covers) ──
# Defaults to ./uploads next to the server. Point this at a persistent volume in production,
# otherwise uploaded images disappear on redeploy while the database keeps referencing them.
# UPLOADS_DIR=/var/lib/ilmnet/uploads

# ── Frontend hosting ──
# The API serves the built single-file frontend from ../dist when it exists.
# SERVE_FRONTEND=false        → API only (host the frontend elsewhere, set VITE_API_URL)
# FRONTEND_DIR=/app/frontend  → override the build location (see docker-compose)
# SERVE_FRONTEND=true
# FRONTEND_DIR=

# ── Seed safety ──
# `npm run seed` is DESTRUCTIVE (wipes content/scholars/subjects and inserts demo data) and
# refuses to run when NODE_ENV=production. Use `npm run seed:reference` on a fresh production
# database (adds subjects + scholars, never touches content).
# SEED_ALLOW_RESET=true  → only to deliberately wipe a production database.
